# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: APPROVED 2026-10-16

An analysis runs only after a person has read this file and approved it. Edit anything;
approval is what freezes it. After approval the plan is the record of intent -- change of
mind means a new analysis, not a quiet edit.

## Goal
Show how the six samples of the rnaseq_bulk assay relate to one another in gene-expression
space: a principal component analysis of the samples from the gene-level counts, plotted
with each sample coloured by its condition (untreated vs treated, from the design table).

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv (`sample_id,condition,group,replicate`; A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) |

The scripts locate both files through `_system/resolve_artifact.py` at run time; no path is
hard-coded. The count matrix has columns `gene_id, gene_name, A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3` and
200 genes. Values are length-scaled (non-integer) counts.

## Method
All steps run in the `gars-bio` environment (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, matplotlib; all
pinned in `_references/gars-bio.lock.txt`), as one script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, submitted
through the executor door. No input is modified.

1. Load the `counts_gene` matrix; index by `gene_id`, keep `gene_name` aside, take the six
   sample columns. Load the design table and check its `sample_id` values match the matrix
   columns exactly; stop with an error if they differ.
2. Drop genes with zero counts in every sample. Report how many genes remain.
3. Transform: `log2(count + 1)`. (The counts are length-scaled and so not integers, and no
   DESeq2-style variance-stabilising transform is applied; log2(x+1) is the simple, stated
   choice. Say if you would rather use a different transform.)
4. Select the 500 most variable genes by variance of the log2 values across samples. With
   only 200 genes in this matrix, all non-zero genes are used, so no selection takes place.
5. Centre each gene to mean zero across samples. Do not scale to unit variance.
6. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))`, via the
   default SVD solver. Record the proportion of variance explained for every component.
7. Plot PC1 (x) against PC2 (y): one point per sample, coloured by `condition`, each point
   labelled with its `sample_id`, axis labels carrying the % variance explained, and a legend
   for condition. Saved as PNG (300 dpi) and SVG.
8. Write the sample scores table (`sample_id, condition, group, replicate, PC1..PC6`) and the
   variance-explained table (`PC, proportion_variance, cumulative`) as CSV.

Nothing else is computed: no clustering, no batch correction, no statistical test of
separation, and no interpretation of the result.

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

| File | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| results/pca_plot.png | figure | PC1 vs PC2 scatter of the samples, coloured by condition, labelled by sample_id |
| results/pca_plot.svg | figure | The same plot, as vector graphics |
| results/pca_scores.csv | table | Per-sample PC scores (PC1-PC6) joined to the design columns |
| results/pca_variance.csv | table | Proportion and cumulative proportion of variance explained per PC |

## Execution
Runs: batch
Expected wall time well under a minute of compute (6 samples x 200 genes); the scheduler
queue wait dominates. Total output size under 1 MB. Submitted via
`python3 _system/executorlib.py submit --workspace projects/run-ccaed574 <script>`.
